.TH SYS_EXIT 2
.SH NAME
sys_exit \- end the calling process (SYS_EXIT)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

void sys_exit(int code);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sys_exit
ends the calling process, all its threads, with exit status
.IR code .
The other threads are woken from whatever they wait in and end on their
way out of the kernel. When the last one is gone, the kernel closes the
process's ports (clients waiting on them get
.BR EPIPE ,
calls still queued
.BR ENOENT ),
frees its memory, and keeps it as a "zombie" until its parent collects
the status with
.BR sys_wait (2).
Its children are adopted by the supervisor,
.BR init (8).
.PP
A status other than 0 (and other than the end by SIGINT or SIGHUP) is
logged by the kernel: "mk: NAME (pid N) exited with CODE".
Returning from
.B main
calls
.B sys_exit
with its value.
.SH RETURN VALUE
It does not return.
.SH NOTES
If the supervisor itself ends, the kernel stops the system (a panic).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sys_wait (2),
.BR thread_start (2),
.BR kill (2),
.BR sys_spawn (2)
